{% comment %}
  Contact form — the single public route for reaching Clippar.

  No email address appears anywhere on the storefront. Every place in the
  policies that used to print one now links here instead, so the address is
  never published, scraped, or stuck in a customer's sent items.

  Uses Shopify's native {% form 'contact' %}, which posts to /contact and
  delivers to the store's sender address configured in Settings → Notifications.
  That address stays server-side.

  Two things matter about the markup:

    * The success and error states live INSIDE the form block —
      form.posted_successfully? and form.errors are only in scope there.
    * Nothing calls preventDefault on the valid path. Shopify injects its
      spam-protection token during the native submit; intercepting it silently
      breaks every real submission with "Missing CAPTCHA token".

  Any contact[Some Label] field is carried into the notification email as a
  labelled line, which is how the enquiry type, order number and free-text
  topic reach the inbox without any custom backend.

  Progressive enhancement: the conditional fields render visible, and
  clippar.js hides them on load. With JS off the form still works — the
  customer just sees two extra optional fields.
{% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  assign heading = section.settings.heading | default: 'Contact us'
  assign body = section.settings.body | default: 'Tell us what you need and a human will answer.'
-%}

<div class="contact-page">
  <div class="form-wrap">
    <div class="form-card contact-card">
      <h1>{{ heading }}</h1>
      <p>{{ body }}</p>
      <div class="form-error" id="contact-error"></div>

      {%- form 'contact', id: 'contact-form', class: 'contact-form' -%}
        {%- if form.posted_successfully? -%}
          <div class="success-state" id="contact-success" style="display:block;">
            <div class="tick">
              <svg width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#a8e63d" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                <path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5"/>
              </svg>
            </div>
            <h3>Message sent.</h3>
            <p>We aim to reply within 2 business days. Safety issues are treated as urgent.</p>
          </div>
        {%- else -%}
          {%- if form.errors -%}
            <div class="form-error" style="display:block;">
              {{ form.errors.messages['form'] | default: 'Something went wrong. Please try again.' }}
            </div>
          {%- endif -%}

          <div class="form-group">
            <label for="c-name">Your name</label>
            <input type="text" id="c-name" name="contact[name]" placeholder="Tiger" required autocomplete="name">
          </div>

          <div class="form-group">
            <label for="c-email">Email address</label>
            <input type="email" id="c-email" name="contact[email]" placeholder="you@gmail.com" required autocomplete="email">
          </div>

          <div class="form-group">
            <label for="c-topic">What's this about?</label>
            <select id="c-topic" name="contact[Enquiry type]" required>
              <option value="" disabled selected>Select a topic</option>
              <option value="App support">App support — something isn't working</option>
              <option value="Order or delivery">Order or delivery</option>
              <option value="Return, refund or fault">Return, refund or faulty item</option>
              <option value="Safety issue (URGENT)">Safety issue with the Mount Kit (urgent)</option>
              <option value="Account, billing or subscription">Account, billing or subscription</option>
              <option value="Privacy or data request">Privacy or data request</option>
              <option value="Press, partnerships or business">Press, partnerships or business</option>
              <option value="Other">Other</option>
            </select>
          </div>

          {%- comment -%}
            Shown by clippar.js only for Other. Optional either way — a customer
            who picks Other and leaves it blank still gets through.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <div class="form-group" id="c-other-group">
            <label for="c-other">Tell us the topic</label>
            <input type="text" id="c-other" name="contact[Other topic]" placeholder="In a few words">
          </div>

          {%- comment -%}
            The refund and shipping policies both ask customers to quote an order
            number. Shown by clippar.js for the order-related topics, so the
            policies have somewhere to point.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <div class="form-group" id="c-order-group">
            <label for="c-order">Order number <span class="optional">optional</span></label>
            <input type="text" id="c-order" name="contact[Order number]" placeholder="#1001">
          </div>

          <div class="form-group">
            <label for="c-body">Message</label>
            <textarea id="c-body" name="contact[body]" rows="6" placeholder="As much or as little detail as you like." required></textarea>
          </div>

          {%- comment -%}
            Honeypot. Positioned off-screen rather than display:none, which is
            trivial for a bot to detect. Named "Comments" because form-spam
            scripts that blanket-fill every input will take it, while browser
            autofill has no reason to touch it — a false positive here would
            silently swallow a real customer's message.

            Not a cure. The bot that reached us picked its fields deliberately,
            so the timing check in clippar.js is the one more likely to catch
            this particular operator.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <div class="hp-field" aria-hidden="true">
            <label for="c-hp">Leave this field empty</label>
            <input type="text" id="c-hp" name="contact[Comments]" tabindex="-1" autocomplete="off">
          </div>

          <button class="btn-submit" type="submit" id="c-submit">SEND MESSAGE</button>
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endform -%}

      <p class="form-note">We aim to reply within 2 business days. For a privacy request we'll respond within 30 days and won't charge you.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

{% stylesheet %}
  /* The section renders inside the theme's CSS grid, where an item defaults to
     min-width:auto and refuses to shrink below its content — the same overflow
     that bit the legal pages on a phone. */
  .contact-page {
    box-sizing: border-box;
    min-width: 0;
    max-width: min(560px, 100%);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 64px 20px 80px;
  }

  .contact-card h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 22px;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    margin-bottom: 10px;
  }

  /* Matches .form-group input/select in clippar.css — kept here because the
     shared sheet has no textarea rule. */
  .contact-card textarea {
    width: 100%;
    padding: 13px 14px;
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.04);
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
    border-radius: 8px;
    color: var(--white);
    font-family: inherit;
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.5;
    resize: vertical;
  }

  .contact-card textarea::placeholder { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.2); }

  .contact-card textarea:focus {
    outline: none;
    border-color: var(--green);
  }

  /* Off-screen rather than display:none or visibility:hidden, both of which a
     bot can cheaply test for before deciding whether to fill a field. */
  .hp-field {
    position: absolute;
    left: -9999px;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .contact-card .optional {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-left: 6px;
  }
{% endstylesheet %}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Contact form",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "heading",
      "label": "Heading",
      "default": "Contact us"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "body",
      "label": "Intro",
      "default": "Tell us what you need and a human will answer."
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
